<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mastino II della Scala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1308">1308</a>–<a href="/years/1351">1351</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mastino II della Scala was lord of Verona from 1329, nephew of Cangrande I. He extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Parma and Lucca, but a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> defeated him in 1336–1339, stripping him of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, Treviso and other cities. He kept Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mastino-ii-della-scala.jpg" alt="Mastino II della Scala" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ugo franchini · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mastino_II_della_Scala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mastino II was the son of Alboino I della Scala and Beatrice da Correggio, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family that ruled Verona. When Cangrande I died, Mastino and his brother Alberto II were both associated with the rule of the city, but Mastino's independent manner soon overshadowed his brother. In 1328 he married Taddea da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, a daughter of Jacopo I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Abandoning the careful policy of balance kept by his predecessors, Mastino turned to conquest in the first part of his rule. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> in 1332, and in 1335 Parma in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and Lucca in Tuscany. The growth of his power drew the other local powers into a league against him: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, Siena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Mastino held out in the first year of the war, but in 1337 the league was joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti">Azzone Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the Este of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, the Gonzaga of Mantua and the Papal States.</p><p>Surrounded on every side, he could only seek peace through the mediation of Emperor Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, which he obtained in 1339. His lands were reduced to Verona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, and the rest was divided among the victors. A later attempt to recover part of what he had lost, using the German mercenaries who had stayed in Vicenza after the war under Lodrisio Visconti, failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mastino died in Verona on 3 June 1351. He was buried in a Gothic mausoleum near the church of Santa Maria Antica, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger-tombs">Scaliger Tombs</a>. His sons Cangrande, Alboino and Cansignorio survived him; Cangrande married Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a daughter of Louis IV, and Cansignorio married Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>. His daughter Verde married Niccolò II d'Este, Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and his daughter Beatrice Regina married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a> on 27 September 1350, ties that joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family to two of the houses that had fought him. Among his illegitimate children, Pietro became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mastino II della Scala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mastino II</b> the lordship reached Lucca and Parma, but a coalition of Venice and Florence stripped most of it away in 1339.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1335" aria-label="Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mastino II della Scala"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 6177h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 6177h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1335">What was here in 1335? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
